WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.676
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.089 --> 00:00:04.380
JASON POLITES: My name's Jason.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.350
I'm a product manager on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.240
And as you can see,
today we're going

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.500
to be talking about building
serverless applications

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.760
with Google Cloud functions.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.940
Before we do that, we
might need to just describe

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.730
what this word, this weird
word "serverless" that we

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.060
see popping up all over
the place actually means.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.350
And you might think that
it means no servers, right?

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.375
Well, kind of.

00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:36.100
It could mean any, one,
or all of these things,

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.870
so no need to manage
servers, no need

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.045
to even think about servers.

00:00:40.045 --> 00:00:42.640
There's a provisioning argument.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.212
Maybe it's about
what you pay for.

00:00:44.212 --> 00:00:45.670
Maybe it's not
about billing, maybe

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.790
it's about developer experience
or the unit of deployment,

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.050
et cetera.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.470
For the purpose
of today, I'm just

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.932
going to narrow that scope.

00:00:56.932 --> 00:00:58.890
So we're going to talk
about these four things.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.610
Serverless by
definition of something

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.879
that you don't even need
to think about servers,

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.480
you pay only for what you
use, it's event-oriented,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.750
and you deploy
functions and not apps.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.500
OK, what does that really mean?

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.970
In order to answer
that question,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.360
I'm going to tell
a little story.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.340
This is Tony, and
Tony has an app.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.610
And he's going to run his
app on a virtual machine.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.190
And so he launches the app,
and the app gets some traffic,

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.860
and his virtual machine
is humming along nicely.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.450
And then his app
gets more popular

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.980
and he gets more traffic.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.480
And now, he can't fit on
a single virtual machine,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.510
but that's cool, he just gets
a second virtual machine.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.180
And the extra traffic that
couldn't get on the first one

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.370
flows over to the second one.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.760
Now, if he's got some sort of
load balancing ahead of this,

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.271
it probably looks more something
like this, and that's fine.

00:02:00.271 --> 00:02:00.770
Sweet.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.810
But there's a problem with
this, and it's these gray boxes.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.610
This means he's
over-provisioned,

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.320
so he's bought and paid for
capacity that he's not using,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.860
and that's costing him money.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.602
Let's see if we can do better.

00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:22.120
We're going to start
with a virtual machine,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.490
but this time, we're not
going to deploy an application

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.031
into that virtual
machine, we're just

00:02:26.031 --> 00:02:28.330
going to deploy a container.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.100
And then when that container
exhausts its resources,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.310
I deploy another container,
and so on and so on and so on.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.260
And then if I'm load
balancing, maybe it

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.480
looks something like that.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.380
So that looks good.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.330
But then what if
I need one more?

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.570
Well, I need a whole
new virtual machine.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.910
So we're kind of
back to square one.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.180
We still have an
over-provisioning problem.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.520
I'm still provisioning
capacity that I'm not using,

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.680
and that's costing me money.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.260
OK, let's try again.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.100
What if we just
remove the server?

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.375
Now when he wants to
add a new container,

00:03:12.375 --> 00:03:15.074
he just adds a new container.

00:03:15.074 --> 00:03:17.890
Sweet.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.960
We still do have some
waste here, though.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:23.590
You can see there's still a
bunch of gray boxes there.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.530
So that's still somewhat
over-provisioned

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.160
and still costing money.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.280
What if we were to shrink
this unit of compute?

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.950
Rather than deploying a
VM image or a container,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.980
what if we made it
as small as possible?

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.670
So each little unit
is going to have

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.080
its own amount of capacity.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.170
And so the amount that we're
over-provisioning is even less.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.836
You're still going
to have a little bit.

00:03:50.815 --> 00:03:53.690
You're not going to be able
to get rid of it entirely,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.259
but this is a much
better place to be.

00:03:56.259 --> 00:03:57.800
And so you end up
in this world where

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.450
you have independent units of
compute, independent functions,

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.710
that scale independently.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.690
And the amount of
over-provisioning

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.640
is as low as it can be.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.250
If I scale
independently, they're

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.130
isolated from one another,
they're stateless,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.500
and they more closely
match the traffic pattern.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.880
So some functions will execute
frequently, some functions not

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.736
so much.

00:04:24.736 --> 00:04:25.236
Sweet.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:33.560
So that's, broadly
speaking, a view

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.830
of computing that's
serverless, and what that means

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.640
and why it's important.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.580
But how do you use this, and
why does this matter to you?

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.800
So just hold that thought.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.452
I'm going to switch gears and
talk about some use cases.

00:04:50.452 --> 00:04:52.160
Imagine you're building
a booking system.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.130
And I apologize that this
is a somewhat dry and boring

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.910
example, but it'll
serve the purpose.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.680
You have some
application, some bit

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.450
of compute, that's
accepting data.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:05.250
Maybe it's accepting orders.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.320
And you want to write those
orders to some back end storage

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:08.820
system.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:13.115
In this case, I'm choosing
Cloud Storage as the example.

00:05:13.115 --> 00:05:15.240
And so that's really the
guts of your application--

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.330
just take orders and
write them to storage.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:19.824
Now you have a new requirement.

00:05:19.824 --> 00:05:21.240
Maybe you want to
do some indexing

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.200
or some processing of this
order before it goes to storage.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.890
And so you implement, in code,
some indexing or some library

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.450
that does this work for you.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.682
But maybe that library or
that code that you've written

00:05:33.682 --> 00:05:35.640
depends on a database,
so it needs to write out

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.350
to a database or an index.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.950
And maybe it needs some special
file or rules to do that.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.949
And probably, it needs some
sort of special authentication.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:44.490
So that's OK.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.310
You put all of that into your
application, and it looks good.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:53.100
Meanwhile, elsewhere, you have
another application, completely

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.550
separate, that's
taking some other data,

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.110
but it needs to do
something similar.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.090
This time, maybe
it's taking invoices

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.950
and it's writing
them to storage.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.065
And then maybe you also need
to index that data as well,

00:06:05.065 --> 00:06:06.690
and you want to reuse
some of this code

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.880
that you've written
over on the order side.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.670
So now, again, you need
the same database network

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.294
and authentication configuration
that you had previously.

00:06:17.294 --> 00:06:17.960
And that's fine.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.920
That will work, and
that happens routinely.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.840
But this might be a problem.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.630
You've duplicated
this configuration

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.380
and these settings and
this authentication

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.320
in two different places, not to
mention duplicating the code.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.200
So what does that mean
for things like security,

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.590
things like deployment?

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.840
If you change this
library, do you

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.060
have to deploy both
versions of your application

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.816
simultaneously?

00:06:42.816 --> 00:06:44.190
Let's see if we
can improve this.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.280
So same application.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.990
Two different sides of
it-- invoices and orders.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.680
This time, we're going to
implement a separate API.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.740
And the applications are
going to call that API

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.050
rather than writing
directly to storage.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.159
And that API is going to
write the file to storage,

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:05.700
and it's going to
do all the indexing

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.910
and have those dependencies.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.590
This is classically
what we might

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.740
term, in a very simplistic way,
a microservices approach, where

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.595
you separate logic into
services rather than baking it

00:07:17.595 --> 00:07:19.520
into the application.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.020
And this is totally fine--

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:25.640
great architecture, works
well, Tony loves it.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.590
There is one small
challenge with this,

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:31.100
and that is that
previously, you recall,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.939
we were writing from the
compute to storage directly.

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:36.980
And the storage, let's
say, was a managed service

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.150
like Cloud Storage.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.350
So it had SLAs and it had all
sorts of uptime guarantees.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.940
And now we've
created our own API

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.760
that we have to scale ourselves
and have to manage ourselves.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.430
And so we've introduced
a point of failure here.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.830
It's not terrible, and there's
many ways to mitigate that,

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.340
but you now do have to
think about how do I

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:56.840
deal with that failure?

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.390
How does the application deal
with the failure to index

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:00.060
the data?

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.280
What does it do?

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.540
So we might be able
to do one step better.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:06.860
Same application.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.570
This time, I'm going to go
back to what I had before.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.540
I'm writing directly to storage.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.850
What if the storage
system itself was aware

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.887
of that write, and it
could emit an event?

00:08:16.887 --> 00:08:19.220
So when the write occurred,
when the mutation in storage

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:20.960
occurred, it would
emit an event,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.390
and then I could attach
code to that event,

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.260
and that code would
have the dependencies.

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.369
So in this model, I've solved,
sort of, two problems in one.

00:08:29.369 --> 00:08:31.410
I've solved the original
problem with duplication

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.059
of configuration in code.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:36.480
And I've also solved the
problem of not depending

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.520
on a managed service and the
reliability challenges that

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.010
might present.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.380
So looks good, right?

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:49.630
Well, there is one
more issue here.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.090
Those two applications
were pretty heavyweight,

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:55.330
and they required virtual
machines under a lot of load.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.307
But this one little piece
that I've pulled out

00:08:57.307 --> 00:08:59.140
doesn't get called all
the time, or maybe it

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.660
doesn't need many resources.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:05.240
So now I'm back to this
over-provisioning situation.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:08.780
If only I had a smaller
unit of compute that I

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.260
could use in place of that.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.832
And the good news is that we do.

00:09:14.832 --> 00:09:15.332
Sweet.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.340
So Google Cloud Functions is
that lightweight, small unit

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.840
of compute.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.650
It's a serverless environment
to build and connect

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.150
cloud services with code.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:34.570
The characteristics of
Cloud Functions are this.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:36.530
You don't need to even
think about service.

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.150
You pay only for what you use.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.130
It is event-oriented, and you
deploy in units of functions,

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.250
not apps.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:44.750
And you'll recall
that this is where

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:49.004
we started in our definition.

00:09:49.004 --> 00:09:51.840
OK, pretty simple
diagram, but just

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.610
to give a sense
of what that looks

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:57.180
like, your cloud environment
will emit events,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:57.839
as I mentioned.

00:09:57.839 --> 00:09:59.880
Mutation on the storage
bucket might be an event,

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:02.730
mutation on a database
might be an event.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.800
Also, external services
will emit events.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.712
Typically, this is materialized
as a webhook callback

00:10:07.712 --> 00:10:08.670
or something like that.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:09.849
So a commit to GitHub.

00:10:09.849 --> 00:10:11.640
And you can configure
that to call you back

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:14.060
with a webhook over HTTP.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:18.354
Your function will come to
life, respond to that event,

00:10:18.354 --> 00:10:19.770
and then from
within the function,

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.990
you can do anything that you
can do in a normal computing

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:22.490
environment.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.132
You can call an API.

00:10:24.132 --> 00:10:25.590
You can write back
to the database.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:26.820
You can call an external API.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.720
In Cloud Functions within
Google, we have two flavors.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.710
An HTTP function is a function
you deploy with an HTTP

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:44.190
trigger, and will give you back,
immediately, a URL with a TLS

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:47.480
certificate so it's secure, and
you can curl that straightaway.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.430
That would be for
synchronous use cases.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:54.185
The webhook callback will
work out-of-the-box for this.

00:10:54.185 --> 00:10:56.060
The second flavor is
the background function.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:57.860
This is where it
happens asynchronously.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:01.820
There's an EventBus in between,
typically, Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.190
This is where you don't
need to know the outcome

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.870
of the function in real time.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:09.200
You're happy for it to be
processed in the background.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.560
And typically, this will be
as a result of an event being

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.119
emitted from a cloud service.

00:11:15.119 --> 00:11:16.660
So let's have a look
at each of these

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.640
in a little bit more detail.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.950
HTTP-- this is the
simplest hello world

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.350
HTTP function you can imagine.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:29.160
We give you an HTTP request
and response object.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.330
This is in Node.

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.910
Anyone familiar with Node--

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.645
the request and response is
literally an Express.js request

00:11:36.645 --> 00:11:38.720
and response.

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:40.890
And so if you've
ever used Express.js,

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.480
then you know how to do this.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.300
Couple of things to point out--

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.940
I just mentioned it's just
a regular request response,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.630
and then you have the full
access to the response object.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.402
You don't need to do any
sort of crazy translations

00:11:54.402 --> 00:11:55.610
on the way in or the way out.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.506
It's just regular, old HTTP.

00:12:01.555 --> 00:12:04.180
This is what a deploy might look
like for that from the command

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:04.679
line.

00:12:04.679 --> 00:12:06.220
We also have a
web UI, which I'll

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:08.470
show you in a little while.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.365
You just deploy the function.

00:12:10.365 --> 00:12:11.740
You give it a
stage bucket, which

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.610
is where we store your
deployment while we're

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.580
building the function.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:18.560
And then you just say
it's a trigger HTTP,

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:19.810
and we'll give you back a URL.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.140
Background functions.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:27.960
Pretty similar.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.460
The only difference
here is instead

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.850
of a request and
response, we give you

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:35.540
an event, which is the event
that was emitted from the cloud

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:36.530
service--

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:39.260
database mutation, storage
mutation, et cetera--

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:40.870
and we give you a callback.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.466
The callback is because in
runtime environments like Node,

00:12:44.466 --> 00:12:45.590
everything is asynchronous.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:48.350
You might be doing some
work asynchronously,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:51.470
and you need to tell us
when your function is done,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:53.390
and so you call the callback.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:56.420
If you don't like using
callbacks, that's cool.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:57.615
You can return a promise.

00:12:57.615 --> 00:12:59.490
In fact, you can just
return a discrete value

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:01.580
and we'll treat it as a promise.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.220
Again, for the people in the
audience familiar with Node

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.677
and JavaScript, this
is particularly useful

00:13:06.677 --> 00:13:08.510
if you're using a third
party library that's

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:09.950
going to return a promise.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:12.062
You don't have to do
any kind of black magic,

00:13:12.062 --> 00:13:13.520
you just return
what they gave you.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.710
And a couple of examples of
deploying background functions.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.710
So one here is triggering
off a Cloud Storage bucket,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.230
the other is triggering
of a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:39.160
Dependencies.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.320
It would be unusual,
I think, for you

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.390
to write a bit of code
that has no dependencies

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:45.223
on any other modules
or any other libraries.

00:13:45.223 --> 00:13:49.090
Typically, you will depend on
numerous libraries of your own,

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.700
or typically, third
party libraries.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.400
In a Node.js environment, they
are expressed in a manifest

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.766
file called a package.json.

00:13:56.766 --> 00:13:58.390
You declare your
dependencies in there,

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:02.140
and then there's a tool called
NPM, Node Package Manager, that

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:04.990
will resolve those dependencies,
download all the files,

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.180
and package up your app.

00:14:09.052 --> 00:14:11.010
That means that you can
do something like this.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:15.080
You can require this external
dependency in your code.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:17.720
And typically, you
would declare it

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:20.325
like this in your package.json.

00:14:20.325 --> 00:14:21.950
So when you deploy
your Cloud Function,

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.140
we will actually run
NPM install for you.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:26.870
So you don't have to
resolve those dependencies

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:29.990
and zip them up into a big
fat ZIP and send them to us.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:32.187
You just send us your source
code in your manifest,

00:14:32.187 --> 00:14:33.270
and we'll handle the rest.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:41.690
And this is what the deploy
command looks like if you're

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:45.420
using a manifest file.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:47.960
It's the same as it was
before, so nothing changes.

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:50.780
We just suck that up and run
NPM install in the cloud.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:56.930
Logging and monitoring.

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:04.832
So from your Cloud
Function, you just

00:15:04.832 --> 00:15:07.040
write a console.log command
as you would do normally,

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.600
as you would do anywhere else.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:11.045
We will capture that and write
it to Stackdriver Logging.

00:15:11.045 --> 00:15:12.545
From Stackdriver
Logging, you can do

00:15:12.545 --> 00:15:14.150
a bunch of interesting things.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.310
You can pipe those
logs to BigQuery,

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:20.030
you can set up a filter to pipe
those logs to a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:25.150
topic and have another function
execute in response to that.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:28.300
If you have an uncalled
exception in your code,

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:30.790
that will get sent to
Stackdriver Errors.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:32.290
From there, you
can set up alerts

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:35.140
to tell you that something's
gone unusually wrong that we

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:38.190
didn't expect with the function.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:42.690
And all of this is available
through CLI, UI, and API.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:47.230
Monitoring.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:51.269
We report the essential
telemetry that you need--

00:15:51.269 --> 00:15:52.810
how many times your
function is being

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:56.000
invoked, how long it ran for,
and how much memory did it use?

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:05.760
OK, what does the environment
look like that we run in?

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.530
This comes up
particularly for those

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.120
who might be running
any native libraries.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:12.330
And by native, I
mean code that needs

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.280
to be compiled against the
underlying operating system.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:21.690
So Cloud Functions run
in an open environment,

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:26.390
meaning that we just have a
vanilla flavor of Debian Linux.

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:28.520
We have a vanilla
flavor of Node.js.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.650
We pre-install ImageMagick
just because that's

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:34.160
a very commonly
used native library.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.080
We give you a local disk, @/tmp.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:40.920
Within the environment,
you're automatically

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.590
authenticated against
other Google services,

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.530
so you don't have to bundle
in any authentication tokens

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:49.410
or consider how you
authenticate your function

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.750
against other services
in your project.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.200
It's automatically done for you.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.900
And the last point is
about the compilation.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.174
Some node modules will
have a native component.

00:16:59.174 --> 00:17:00.590
And when you run
NPM install, it's

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:02.510
going to compile
that native code.

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:04.849
If you run NPM install and
it compiles native code

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:08.690
on your Mac laptop
and then you deploy

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:14.150
to a non-Mac environment,
you might have a bad day.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:16.310
So the advantage of
having NPM install

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:20.569
run for you is that you just
have to name the dependency.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:23.260
We will run NPM install, we
will run the compilation step,

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.760
and by definition, it's going
to run on the same environment

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:27.343
that we're going to
execute on, so you

00:17:27.343 --> 00:17:29.930
don't run into these
crazy incompatibilities.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:33.170
If the node module you're
using doesn't prepackage

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:36.470
a compilation step, and it
depends on some library that

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:41.750
is not part of the node
module, 9 times out of 10,

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:44.150
you can go and download
the precompiled Debian

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:46.060
version of that library.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:49.380
And because it's just a
standard Debian environment,

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:50.964
that should work.

00:17:50.964 --> 00:17:52.380
If you really,
really, really have

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:57.090
to compile your own library onto
the image, onto the same image,

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.520
then you don't get
this image from us,

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:01.080
you just go get it
from the internet.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.082
Local development.

00:18:08.082 --> 00:18:08.790
So this is great.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:10.956
I've got this environment,
I'm writing my functions,

00:18:10.956 --> 00:18:13.010
I'm deploying them,
I'm executing them,

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.587
but it's a little
slow in the sense

00:18:14.587 --> 00:18:16.670
that I have to go through
the whole deploy process

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:19.700
every time I want
to make a change.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:24.270
It'd be great if we gave you a
local emulation environment so

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.600
that you can build and
test and iterate and debug

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:30.330
your functions locally
before you deploy them.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.440
So this is available today.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:33.900
Just NPM install the emulator.

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:39.429
It runs a simulated environment
of the cloud environment

00:18:39.429 --> 00:18:40.470
for your Cloud Functions.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:43.140
It has exactly the
same API surface area.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:45.360
So if you wanted to
write tooling around

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.210
how to deploy
functions using an API,

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.100
that tooling will work
with the emulator.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:55.530
So you can easily switch
between emulator and live code.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.030
And you can debug with this.

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.910
So it has a command to
enable the debugger.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.730
And then you can attach
your favorite IDE to that

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.480
and step through
your JavaScript code.

00:19:10.015 --> 00:19:11.890
Deployment I already
touched on a little bit,

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:13.960
but I want to walk you
through how this works.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:18.050
So you can deploy inline.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:21.110
We have an inline
code editor in the UI.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:25.470
Or you can deploy via the
command line or via an API.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.000
If you're deploying directly
from your local file system,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.711
we'll zip it up for you
without the dependencies,

00:19:30.711 --> 00:19:32.210
or with the
dependencies if you want

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:37.297
to send them with us for
whichever reason you like.

00:19:37.297 --> 00:19:38.880
And we'll send it
to the API and we'll

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.696
run the build
process from there.

00:19:40.696 --> 00:19:43.070
If you don't want to deploy
directly from your local file

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:48.650
system, you can also deploy
from your GitHub or BitBucket

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:50.540
repository.

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:53.390
This is done using a tool called
Cloud Source Repositories.

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:56.810
We'll automatically keep in
sync your Cloud repository

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.455
with your GitHub repository.

00:19:58.455 --> 00:20:00.830
And so you're actually deploying
from Cloud Source repos,

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:02.855
but it is kept in
sync with GitHub,

00:20:02.855 --> 00:20:04.730
so you don't really have
to interact with it.

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:10.940
You just commit to GitHub,
and then trigger a deploy,

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.560
and your Cloud Source
repo will sync.

00:20:18.337 --> 00:20:20.420
So I want to talk a little
bit about what actually

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.280
happens at deploy time.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:23.910
So you've written your code.

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:25.320
Let's say you're using the CLI.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:29.170
You've gone G Cloud,
beta functions, deploy,

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.350
and then what happens?

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:34.630
So you hit Deploy.

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:38.200
As I said, we zip it
up if it's in the CLI.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.780
When it gets to the
server, we run NPM install.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:46.420
If there are already
packages there,

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.700
then we might just
run NPM rebuild

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:50.655
to capture any native
compilation that needs

00:20:50.655 --> 00:20:51.780
to happen for the platform.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:57.110
We have, internally, a
thing called create version.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:59.090
So what this is doing
is saying, OK, we

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:00.799
have a new version
of this function.

00:21:00.799 --> 00:21:02.840
I'm going to create a new
version so the existing

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:04.025
version continues to exist.

00:21:06.554 --> 00:21:08.970
And before we do anything, I'm
going to health check that.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:12.040
I'm going to make sure
that that thing is OK.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:13.950
That means that the
environment's spun up

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:17.400
correctly, the image is
being created correctly,

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.710
the runtime has
started correctly.

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:22.380
We can't check that your
function is going to work,

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.910
because your function, of
course, may have side effects.

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:26.285
So we can't invoke
your function,

00:21:26.285 --> 00:21:30.420
but we can do everything
but that to make sure

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:32.230
that everything's OK.

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:34.660
And if everything's OK,
we'll mark the deployment

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:36.892
as complete, and
then we'll gradually

00:21:36.892 --> 00:21:39.100
move traffic from the old
version to the new version.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.302
OK, so that all sounds great.

00:21:46.302 --> 00:21:47.010
Now what do I do?

00:21:49.857 --> 00:21:51.690
One of these cases that
pops up quite a lot,

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:55.470
and I've touched on it a few
times, is lightweight ETL.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:59.967
So a file comes in, hits
storage or comes into the cloud,

00:21:59.967 --> 00:22:01.800
and you want to do some
transformation of it

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:03.370
before it goes somewhere else.

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:07.800
So one solution to that is
have the file hit storage

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:13.360
immediately, have storage
call a Cloud Function,

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.970
and maybe from there, you want
to write it to a database.

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:19.262
Or maybe you want to
send it to BigQuery.

00:22:19.262 --> 00:22:21.220
Or maybe you just want
to write back to storage

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:22.930
with a mutated
version of that file.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.520
This comes up in things
like image processing,

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:32.110
video processing, indexing, like
in my earlier example, entity

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:33.730
extraction.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.430
You might want to send the
image to the vision API

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:38.344
to pull the entities out of it.

00:22:38.344 --> 00:22:39.760
Content sanitation
and filtering--

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.740
those sorts of things.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.420
Semios is one of the
customers we have

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:46.682
using Cloud Functions for this.

00:22:46.682 --> 00:22:48.390
They're in here because
I thought they're

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:49.940
a really interesting use case.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:54.740
They have around 150,000
sensors in orchards

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:59.090
tracking things like temperature
and pressure, humidity,

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:00.192
those sorts of things.

00:23:00.192 --> 00:23:02.150
And they aggregate all
that data in the orchard

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:03.694
and send it back to the cloud.

00:23:03.694 --> 00:23:06.110
And it passes through a Cloud
Function on its way through,

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:07.776
and through that Cloud
Function, they're

00:23:07.776 --> 00:23:10.507
doing some filtering before
it gets to the database.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:18.970
Another use case we see a
lot, and I also alluded to,

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:21.620
is microservices and webhooks.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.230
So there's a few different
flavors of how this might work.

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:31.120
One is that the client is
calling, typically via HTTP,

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.327
the Cloud Function directly.

00:23:32.327 --> 00:23:33.910
So the client is
outside of the cloud,

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:35.659
the Cloud Function is
inside of the cloud,

00:23:35.659 --> 00:23:37.300
and they just call it directly.

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:39.400
You can think of this
as a microservice.

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:41.770
The client may also be inside
the cloud in this case.

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:45.770
API.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:47.720
So similar to a
microservice, but it's

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:53.210
where the client is
calling the Cloud Function

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:54.819
and treating it as an API.

00:23:54.819 --> 00:23:56.360
In the same way that
you would if you

00:23:56.360 --> 00:24:01.800
were to build a website
with exposed endpoints,

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.760
the mobile client is calling
directly to the Cloud Function.

00:24:08.740 --> 00:24:09.460
Webhook.

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:12.010
This is the example
I gave before where

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:14.740
somebody like a GitHub
might be calling you back,

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.464
or maybe you're sending
an SMS using Twilio,

00:24:17.464 --> 00:24:18.880
and they're going
to call you back

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:20.200
with a webhook when it's done.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:25.910
So microservices,
data ingestion APIs,

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:28.910
callbacks from
external services.

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:32.460
A good example here is Vroom.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:37.530
Vroom are the largest
used car seller on eBay.

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:39.230
And they accept
data from partners.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:40.599
They have a network of partners.

00:24:40.599 --> 00:24:41.640
They want to accept data.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:46.110
They're using Cloud Functions
as a data ingestion mechanism,

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:47.910
as an API for their
partners to call.

00:24:51.399 --> 00:24:53.190
And the last one I want
to talk about today

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:55.920
is more of a fun use case.

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:57.040
So bots an action.

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:01.200
So this is where some messaging
client, some chat client,

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:05.850
some voice-enabled device is
going to use a Cloud Function

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.190
to create a custom command--

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:12.295
something to extend the
behavior of this tool.

00:25:12.295 --> 00:25:13.670
So they'll use
the Cloud Function

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:19.160
for doing this-- typically,
again, enabled over HTTP.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.100
Messaging bots-- things
like adding custom actions

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:26.800
to Google Assistant
and home automation.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:28.750
So the example here is Meetup.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:30.790
They created a
custom Slack command.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.220
And so when you type this
Slack command into their Slack

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:35.650
channel, it will fire
a file function that

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:37.610
will create a ticket in JIRA.

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:41.230
It's part of their way to
automate their development

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:41.900
processes.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:50.920
This is my own artistic
talent being shown.

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:52.180
It's supposed to be a drum.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.371
And then my wife said that it
looked like a bowl of rice.

00:25:55.371 --> 00:25:56.870
It's supposed to
be a drum, and it's

00:25:56.870 --> 00:25:59.950
supposed to remind me that
this is the drum roll.

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:05.550
You may have already heard, but
we also now support Firebase.

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:07.680
So you can cause
a Cloud Function

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:11.940
to be invoked from a mutation in
the Firebase real time database

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:14.820
from life cycle events
on authentication,

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:18.659
and also from analytics events
from your mobile client.

00:26:18.659 --> 00:26:20.700
But I don't want to steal
their thunder too much.

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:28.550
They have a session
today at 4 o'clock.

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:30.890
I encourage you to go
and have a look at that.

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.215
And you can see here
just those examples

00:26:33.215 --> 00:26:34.340
in the context of Firebase.

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:37.130
So processing payments
through an external provider,

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:40.700
sending an email or an SMS,
backend service integration,

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:44.390
so connecting to the
rest of the cloud.

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:48.140
And then funnel optimization
is leveraging things

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:51.890
like analytics to know what
the end users are doing,

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:53.780
and to react to that with code.

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:57.290
So a great example might be,
depending what the end user is,

00:26:57.290 --> 00:27:01.490
doing, you might want to send
them a push notification when

00:27:01.490 --> 00:27:03.640
they enter or exit a
particular analytics cohort.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:12.530
So Cloud Functions
is in beta today.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:15.090
It's available for
