00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:19.360
Just go to that URL and
you can find out more.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.880
That's all I have
in terms of content.

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:24.458
Does anyone want to see a demo?

00:27:24.458 --> 00:27:28.186
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:29.124 --> 00:27:30.540
Let me rephrase
that-- does anyone

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.535
want to see me try
to do a demo and have

00:27:32.535 --> 00:27:37.490
it fail catastrophically
and be embarrassed?

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:38.240
Yeah, more people.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.270
Great.

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:42.910
OK, switch to demo, please.

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:44.874
OK, this looks confusing.

00:27:44.874 --> 00:27:46.040
I'm zoomed out a little bit.

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.581
That's why the boxes
are [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:47.581 --> 00:27:49.868
Let me see if I can make
that a little bit better.

00:27:52.616 --> 00:27:53.490
That's worse.

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:55.550
OK, we'll live with it.

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:57.910
OK, what are you
looking at here?

00:27:57.910 --> 00:27:59.920
All these gray
boxes at the top--

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:04.960
you can think of those as
users calling your function.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:09.700
Now, unfortunately, when
we were creating this demo,

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:12.250
we thought it would be easy
just to run it in a browser.

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:14.920
It turns out the browser
is not great at generating

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.010
many, many concurrent
HTTP requests.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:21.700
So even though there's, I think,
about 80 boxes on the screen

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.035
there, we're not talking
80 concurrent requests.

00:28:24.035 --> 00:28:25.660
They're just going
to fire off requests

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:28.000
as quickly as they can.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:31.430
Each little box
represents a request.

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:33.017
When the Cloud
Function executes,

00:28:33.017 --> 00:28:34.350
it's just going to do one thing.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.260
It's just going
to return a color.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.440
And the box is going to render
the color that it returns,

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:42.010
and that's it.

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.020
Once we kick this off,
we'll see these boxes

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:46.087
switching color
to whatever color

00:28:46.087 --> 00:28:47.170
the function is returning.

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:49.480
It's returning the same
color for every one.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:51.406
Then I'm going to show
you the deploy process.

00:28:51.406 --> 00:28:53.030
Live, I'm going to
redeploy a function,

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:55.480
I'm going to change the
color, and visually, you

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:58.690
should see all these boxes
flick over to the new color.

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:02.440
And down below, what
you'll see is the latency--

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:06.546
the median response time
from executing this function.

00:29:06.546 --> 00:29:07.670
So let's see if this works.

00:29:11.044 --> 00:29:16.210
OK, these are all my
boxes changing color.

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.400
And below, you can
see the latency.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:24.210
Now, I'm going switch
over to my Cloud console.

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:25.560
Here's my send color function.

00:29:28.344 --> 00:29:29.698
I'm just going to edit.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:36.240
So you can see here, this is
where I'm sending the response.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.660
It's just an HTTP function.

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.210
I've got some call
stuff there just

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:40.751
to make it callable
from the browser.

00:29:40.751 --> 00:29:43.340
Just ignore that.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:49.552
So I'm going to change this to
send green rather than blue.

00:29:49.552 --> 00:29:52.107
Hit Save.

00:29:52.107 --> 00:29:53.690
And recalling what
I mentioned before,

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:58.430
that's going to send that
deployment to the cloud.

00:29:58.430 --> 00:30:00.780
It's going to run NPM install.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:02.100
It's going to build an image.

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:03.620
It's going to create a version--

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:06.420
I better switch across
so you can see it.

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.510
It's going to create a version.

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:10.500
It's going to health
check that version.

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:12.660
And assuming the
health check passes

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:17.280
and everything's OK, that will
become the current version.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.790
And right on cue--

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:25.614
right on cue-- it's
going to change.

00:30:25.614 --> 00:30:28.988
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:39.240
So that's just showing how easy
it is to deploy a function.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.530
And if you had some
error, some problem in it,

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:43.980
the existing function
keeps ticking.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:45.900
And we will health
check everything,

00:30:45.900 --> 00:30:48.390
and then we'll switch
across to the new version.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:49.860
And you'll notice
that the colors

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:51.540
didn't flick immediately.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:53.430
That's more a
browser issue, but it

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:57.180
may be that there's
traffic, rather

00:30:57.180 --> 00:30:59.040
requests, that are
currently being served

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.680
will complete before they
get to the new version.

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:04.650
So you might get some
timing issue in that,

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:06.420
and you saw that
reflected there.

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.720
OK, so that's all I have today.

